{
  "case_id": "2009-08-1200",
  "document_hash": "2d15c865f973676d17d248db7aa0a776738dbb71ba25c8895b7385c2b8f1c68f",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Kevin K. Russell",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1200",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2d15c865f973676d17d248db7aa0a776738dbb71ba25c8895b7385c2b8f1c68f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2d15c865f973-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. RUSSELL: Mr. Chief Justice, and may it\nplease the Court:\nCongress rarely makes ignorance of the law a\ndefense to civil liability, and the Fair Debt Collection\nPractices Act is no exception to that rule. While it\nmay seem unfair to hold defendants in some sense\nstrictly liable for legal mistakes in the civil context,\nthe accumulated wisdoms of generations of legal practice\nhas been that attempting to fix that unfairness through\na mistake of law defense causes more harm than it\nprevents."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1200",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2d15c865f973676d17d248db7aa0a776738dbb71ba25c8895b7385c2b8f1c68f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2d15c865f973-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: I really don't think that the -- the word that we're wrestling with here is the -- the word \"intentional.\" It seems to me it's the word \"violation,\" because violation -- when it says the violation was intentional or unintentional, does \"violation\" mean the act that constitutes a violation of the law, or does \"violation\" mean the -- the fact of violating the law?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1200",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2d15c865f973676d17d248db7aa0a776738dbb71ba25c8895b7385c2b8f1c68f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2d15c865f973-0001-0000",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Ellis v. United States, 206 U.S. 246. The relevant passage is at page 257. It's a 1907 opinion from Justice Holmes."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1200",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2d15c865f973676d17d248db7aa0a776738dbb71ba25c8895b7385c2b8f1c68f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2d15c865f973-0001-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: Well, maybe your colleague can, but how -- so they define a -- the reasonable error falls in is because he's a person who regularly collects or attempts to collect consumer debts owed to a -- you know, owed to another person. That's pretty broad."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States (amicus curiae)",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1200",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2d15c865f973676d17d248db7aa0a776738dbb71ba25c8895b7385c2b8f1c68f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2d15c865f973-0001-0001",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I would like, if I may, to pick up with a point that Mr. Russell made, which is that the FDCPA is of a piece with most civil regulatory statutes, in that it makes knowledge of the law an aggravating factor and subjects violators who know that they are violating a law subject to substantial civil penalties of up to $16,000 per day. But it doesn't completely excuse violations based on a lack of knowledge of the law. In order to be excused, the violation must have resulted from a bona fide error and meet all three elements of the bona fide error defense."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Coakley",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1200",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2d15c865f973676d17d248db7aa0a776738dbb71ba25c8895b7385c2b8f1c68f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2d15c865f973-0002-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "opponent here has suggested that the way to deal with the lawyer's problem that I mentioned is simply read the substantive provisions, so that there is no violation for ordinary legal activity where they have gone and asked the FTC or you know, all the things that are against them? What you would have to do. That isn't so hard to do as I first thought, because the words that seem to make -- the primary words of what is outlawed are the words \"unfair or unconscionable means.\""
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Coakley",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1200",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2d15c865f973676d17d248db7aa0a776738dbb71ba25c8895b7385c2b8f1c68f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2d15c865f973-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. COAKLEY: I believe that the lawyer would be just subject to the rigors of the bona fide error defense, the way any other debt collector as defined from 1977 forward would be."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1200",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2d15c865f973676d17d248db7aa0a776738dbb71ba25c8895b7385c2b8f1c68f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2d15c865f973-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: Well, why wouldn't one say, now, Congress has confirmed that the word in the Truth and Lending Act means thus and so, Congress has said that's what those words mean, and then go back to the other statutes and say the same language was used, so it means the same thing, the same thing that Congress has just confirmed?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1200",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2d15c865f973676d17d248db7aa0a776738dbb71ba25c8895b7385c2b8f1c68f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2d15c865f973-0002-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 47,
        "start": 47
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: Do you think that a -- a mistake of law has to be substantively reasonable in order to fall within this defense, and if so, where do you get that? Where do you find that in the statute?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1200",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2d15c865f973676d17d248db7aa0a776738dbb71ba25c8895b7385c2b8f1c68f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2d15c865f973-0002-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 54,
        "start": 54
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Mr. Coakley, why do you need a safe harbor if there is no store? I mean what is the purpose of -- of having this procedure, however inefficient it may be, what is the purpose of having it unless you were going to be liable if you make a mistake of law?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Coakley",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1200",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2d15c865f973676d17d248db7aa0a776738dbb71ba25c8895b7385c2b8f1c68f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2d15c865f973-0002-0006",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 55,
        "start": 55
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the safe harbor is not a --a statutory interpretation why you should drive a meaning into the bona fide error provision that is not apparent on its face. I would say in closing that Justice Breyer, your -- your comments earlier are just right. This is worse -- a reading, the safe harbor, reading the bona fide error provision to exclude legal error is worse than unfair."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Coakley",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1200",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "2d15c865f973676d17d248db7aa0a776738dbb71ba25c8895b7385c2b8f1c68f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2d15c865f973-0003-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 56,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "question about unprecedented. In -- in truth, what would really be unprecedented here would be for this Court to construe the bona fide error defense so that a lawyer or a debt collector who is giving advice to his client to follow a particular way of action, and the law was unsettled, for that lawyer to be subject to -- to be punished for personal liability. And we think that the bona fide error clearly under Lamie encompasses --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1200",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "2d15c865f973676d17d248db7aa0a776738dbb71ba25c8895b7385c2b8f1c68f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2d15c865f973-0003-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 56,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "There are severe limits on the liability, aren't there? Isn't there a thousand -- isn't it a $1,000 limit?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Coakley",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1200",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "2d15c865f973676d17d248db7aa0a776738dbb71ba25c8895b7385c2b8f1c68f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2d15c865f973-0003-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 56,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "This is definitely a class action. This is a class action that wants all of the validation notices that were sent out by this law firm Alderson Reporting Company"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Coakley",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1200",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "2d15c865f973676d17d248db7aa0a776738dbb71ba25c8895b7385c2b8f1c68f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2d15c865f973-0003-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 57,
        "start": 57
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the bona fide error defense is the shield, and we ask the court to affirm the judgment of the Sixth Circuit Court of Appeals."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2009-08-1200",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "2d15c865f973676d17d248db7aa0a776738dbb71ba25c8895b7385c2b8f1c68f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2d15c865f973-0003-0004",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 57,
        "start": 57
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The case is submitted."
    },
